package com.acme.billing;

import org.springframework.http.HttpStatus;
import org.springframework.http.ResponseEntity;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.ExceptionHandler;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestControllerAdvice;

@RestControllerAdvice
public class BillingExceptionHandler {

    @ExceptionHandler(InvoiceNotFoundException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiResponse<Void>> handleNotFound(InvoiceNotFoundException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
                .body(ApiResponse.error("INVOICE_NOT_FOUND", ex.getMessage()));
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(InvoiceNotCancellableException.class)
    public ResponseEntity<ApiResponse<Void>> handleNotCancellable(InvoiceNotCancellableException ex) {
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CONFLICT)
                .body(ApiResponse.error("INVOICE_NOT_CANCELLABLE", ex.getMessage()));
    }
}
```

Conventions applied:
- **Service and errors:** the ownership and unpaid checks are in the service, which throws domain exceptions. A `@RestControllerAdvice` maps them to `ApiResponse.error`, with no try/catch in the controller.
- **Injection and DTOs:** dependencies are `private final` fields set by hand-written constructors. The response is a record in `InvoiceDtos`, wrapped in `ApiResponse.ok`.

An invoice that belongs to someone else returns the same 404 as a missing one, so customers can't probe which invoice ids exist. A paid or already-cancelled invoice returns 409.

